// @no-instantiate
// TODO(miscompile): two closure arms whose PARAMETER object types are field-permuted check
// clean (`vl check --codegen` rc 0) and emit a module that does not instantiate.
//
// THE PREVIOUS DIAGNOSIS IN THIS HEADER WAS WRONG IN BOTH HALVES and is replaced. It read
// the failure as a nullability mismatch on the union-box carrier, and it called the
// PERMUTATION incidental. The disassembly says otherwise on the first count and a two-line
// control says otherwise on the second. Re-derived against a master-seed compiler, not
// inferred — and #1863's own README note that "TWO HEADERS WERE WRONG ABOUT THEIR OWN
// DEFECT" now covers three.
//
// IT IS NOT A NULLABILITY BUG. `wasm-tools validate` reports `expected (ref null $type),
// found (ref $type)` because the printer ELIDES both type indices; they are different
// types, not one type at two nullabilities — the same elision that misled this kind's other
// map fixture. The instruction is `struct.get 1 0` at offset 0x1a9 applied to param 2,
// which the function signature declares `(ref 5)`. Type 1 is the union BOX
// `(struct (field i32) (field anyref))` and type 5 is the CLOSURE fat pointer
// `(struct (field structref) (field i32))`. So the BODY reads a union-box tag off a value
// the SIGNATURE types as a bare closure.
//
// THE PARAMETER REP IS SELF-CONSISTENT END TO END, AND THAT IS THE WHOLE POINT — it is the
// `is` lowering, alone, that disagrees. Delete the two `is` tests and the identical program
// compiles, validates and RUNS:
//
//   function pm(x: FA | FB) { print(x({a: 1, b: "z"})) }   // prints 1
//
// The caller passes a bare closure, the body calls it, nothing is boxed anywhere. Add ONE
// `is` test back and the module is invalid. So the collapse of `FA | FB` to a single
// closure rep is not the defect; `emitIs` still believing the receiver is a box is.
//
// WHY THE TWO HALVES DISAGREE — TWO `TyObj` ARMS, ONE SORTS AND ONE DOES NOT.
//   · The REP layer collapses the union: `repCanonIdGo`'s `TyObj` arm SORTS the (name-sid,
//     child-id) pairs — that sort IS the 2026-08 ruling, added by the change that closed
//     this kind's first member (`types/permuted-named-alias-shares-row.vl`). So `(PA)=>i32`
//     and `(PB)=>i32` share a canon id, the union has one distinct member, and the param
//     valtype is the bare closure.
//   · The TAG layer does not: `cloArmTagOfTy` keys through `cloSigTagSlot(tyToEmitName(ty))`,
//     and `tyToEmitNameAt`'s `TyObj` arm walks `objFieldNames` in DECLARATION order with no
//     sort. The two aliases therefore RENDER differently, intern two signature slots, and
//     the `is` tests compile to two distinct box-tag compares (11 and 15 in the module).
//   `repCanonKeyGo`'s `TyObj` arm — the documented twin of `repCanonIdGo`, the two proved to
//   partition identically over 283,052 comparisons — also walks declaration order. Whoever
//   fixes this should check whether that pair still agrees on a permuted object.
//
// THE PERMUTATION IS THE TRIGGER FOR *THIS FILE*. Give the two parameter objects the SAME
// field order and the renders agree, one slot is interned, and the module VALIDATES — and
// then prints NOTHING where both tests should be true, because `monoStaticIsResult`
// const-folds the guard to `i32.const 0`. That silently-wrong-value tier is ISSUE #1864 and
// is being fixed separately.
//
// SO THE TWO CANNOT LAND APART, and this is the sentence that schedules the work. Fixing
// #1864 alone leaves this file invalid (two tags, one rep). Sorting the render alone makes
// this file VALIDATE and answer NO to both arms — trading check-clean invalid wasm for a
// silent wrong value, which is the strictly WORSE tier, and #1863's README says so. Land
// the render agreement and #1864's fold together, or neither.
//
// A THIRD, WORSE, UNFILED SHAPE FOUND WHILE RE-DERIVING THIS ONE — no functions anywhere:
//
//   type PA = { a: i32, b: string }
//   type PB = { b: string, a: i32 }
//   function g(u: PA | PB) { if u is PA { print(1) } ; if u is PB { print(2) } }
//   g({ a: 5, b: "z" })
//
// `vl check` rc 0, a VALID module, exit 0, and it prints `1` — `u is PB` answers FALSE for a
// value whose type PB IS. Pre-existing on master. The module carries TWO struct rows with
// TWO field ORDERS (`(mut i32)(mut (ref 5))` beside `(mut (ref 5))(mut i32)`), so
// `buildStructTwins` compared them and its second gate refused: `structFieldCodesEq` is
// POSITIONAL, and the layout canonicalization the 2026-08 change added for named
// declarations does not reach a UNION ARM's row. That change measured 14 positions and
// reports all 14 running; the union ARM is a 15th it did not reach. It has no fixture kind
// here either (`@no-instantiate` cannot pin a valid module and `@trap` rejects it), which is
// the same gap this header already recorded for #1864 — one kind is missing, and it would
// serve both. Binding the value first (`const y: PB = {…}; g(y)`) instead fails LOUDLY
// (`emitProgram: ref valtype with no interned shape`), also on master.
//
// The directive is bare: the two engines that run this corpus word the rejection differently
// and share no substring, so this pins the FACT (the module does not instantiate) and leaves
// the wording to the engine.
//
// When it closes, flip to `@run` with `@log A isFA` / `@log A isFB` / `@log B isFA` /
// `@log B isFB` — under the ruling BOTH calls should report BOTH arms, since the two
// signatures are the same type.
type PA = { a: i32, b: string }
type PB = { b: string, a: i32 }
type FA = (PA) => i32
type FB = (PB) => i32

function pm(lbl: string, x: FA | FB) {
  if x is FA { print(lbl + " isFA") }
  if x is FB { print(lbl + " isFB") }
}

function fa(v: PA) { v.a }
function fb(v: PB) { v.a }

pm("A", fa)
pm("B", fb)
